# pcrec-bench O-31 finding F1: a pattern whose own bytes contain a
# comment-closing STAR THEN SLASH must compile and match correctly. The DFA
# emitter's per-state "shortest input" legend used to write a byte-class
# representative straight into a `/* ... */` block comment with no check
# for the comment-closing sequence, so a pattern whose bytes match this
# file's own witness -- the same shape as pcrec-bench's WAF witness
# (wild-waf-crs-942500-comment-obfuscation, `/*!*/` SQL-comment
# obfuscation) -- corrupted every byte the emitter wrote afterward and the
# generated C failed to compile. See src/gen/emit_dfa.c's
# emit_comment_safe_byte and tests/codegen/run_comment_escape.sh's
# structural check for the mechanism; this file is the corpus's own
# regression net -- if the escaping regresses, these blocks stop
# compiling entirely rather than merely answering wrong, exactly the
# failure mode a corpus expectation must survive.

pattern a\/\*!\*\/b
m "a/*!*/b" 0 7
m "xa/*!*/bx" 1 8
n "a/*!*b"

# The raw two-byte sequence lives in the PATTERN'S OWN SOURCE TEXT here
# (not only in a derived state-legend example), exercising the top-of-file
# banner comment and the orientation-block map paragraph as well.
pattern a[*/]b
m "a*b" 0 3
m "a/b" 0 3
n "axb"
